  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CONSERVATION OF SHADE COFFEE PLANTATIONS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Shade-grown coffee plantations are key agro-ecosystems that offer shelter, food and habitat for Neotropical migratory bird species to carry on breeding functions during their migration. Nevertheless, a simplification in trees used for shade and a change to sun-grown coffee has been occurring in Veracruz; additionally, chemical herbicide, fertilizer usage, and other elements degrade habitat quality and threaten bird health. This project plans to increase key habitat protection by 500 hectares as well as strengthen areas from Phase I with the development of the Conservation Investment Strategy. This project will generate important information for protecting habitat with a focus on the landscape including shade-grown coffee plantations and surrounding forest patches. It will promote connectivity between shade-grown coffee farms. Plantations, riparian corridors and live-fences will be diversified and enhanced with the production of 70,000 trees for 30 key species for Neotropical migratory birds. Habitat enhancement will involve 20 schools from coffee-producing municipalities with the &apos;Green School&apos; program. The project aims to strengthen capacities of 190 persons in total (50 producers, 20 young people, 20 community nursery staff, and 100 students) by implementing Neotropical migratory bird conservation practices in shade-grown coffee plantations and educational spaces. The project will increase the portfolio of &apos;Bird Friendly Fincas&apos; or ranches by certifying 50 producers and 100 hectares utilizing sustainable practices.</narrative>
  </description>
